    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1008">

                <pb facs="0386" n="1768" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v13p2/pg1768"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.00/6–3054: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Dillon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-06-30T19:00:00+01:00">June 30, 1954—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5117. Repeated information Saigon 634, Geneva 449. Department limit distribution.
                    Eyes only McClintock Saigon, Johnson Geneva. <pb facs="0387" n="1769"
                        xml:id="pg1769"/>
                    Jebb came to see me this morning after his interview with
                        Mendes-France in the course of which
                    he delivered reply to French <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> of June
                    26 (Deptel 4853)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1008-f1"/> in the form of <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >aide-mémoire</hi>, followed by verbal comments along lines outlined in
                    paragraph 2 reference telegram.</p>
                <p>Jebb reported that Mendes was pleased with text reply and stated that
                        Parodi would give us definite French position in day or
                    so. Mendes also confirmed that
                    negotiations with Viet Minh at Geneva were stalled (Geneva Secto 544, repeated Paris as 519).<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d1008-f2"/></p>
                <p>Mendes was somewhat perplexed by reference
                    to “line running generally west from Dong Hoi” as possible partition line for
                    while Dong Hoi is approximately at 17.5 degrees French had been holding out for
                    18th parallel in face Viet Minh wanting 13th.</p>
                <p>On question of elections, Jebb quoted Mendes as stating that the Viet Minh wanted
                    them to be held in six months but that the French were taking the position that
                    they should be delayed for a year after final settlement and withdrawal of
                    troops had been achieved. This, as presently envisaged, would mean a year and
                    half to two years from now.</p>
                <p>As reported Embtel 5099,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d1008-f3"/> we delivered reply to <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> to Foreign Office this morning.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dillon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
